{
  "configuration_id": "config_010",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_006 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, ViT-L, concat) achieved 0.535 acc / 0.519 F1, a clear ~5-point gain over the RNA-only baseline (config_026, 0.485 acc). This confirms the image modality adds value, but all completed experiments used singleprofile. The multiprofile axis is entirely untested and represents a major structural design choice: whether pooling information across multiple profiles (with simple avgpool aggregation) can further improve representation. Question: Does multiprofile + avgpool aggregation, holding RNA encoder (pca_n2000), image encoder (ViT-L), and fusion (concat) constant, outperform the singleprofile baseline? Why this experiment: config_010 is the minimal, single-axis extension of the best-performing config_006, changing only the profile setting (and its necessary companion, a simple aggregation). It isolates the multiprofile effect without confounding with learned encoders, transformer aggregation, or alternative fusion. The answer will determine whether the large multiprofile design space (configs with transformer/avgpool aggregation, weighted fusion, etc.) is worth further budget, making it the highest-information-per-experiment choice under the limited budget."
}

